WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.468
-Hey, Sean. Welcome to the show.
How are you?

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:04.037 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Very well. How are you?
-I'm doing very good.

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:07.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are reports that you
recently got married.

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:09.709 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Is this true?

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:13.213 align:start size:94% position:6%
Congratulations!
[ Chuckles ]

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:15.081 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
How recent is it?

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:18.485 size:75% position:38% line:92%
-It's, um, Thursday.

00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:19.252 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Oh, my goodness.

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:22.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
So this is, uh -- you're reall
fresh into it.

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:23.656 line:92%
-Yeah, we did a COVID wedding.

00:00:23.656 --> 00:00:27.861 align:start size:94% position:6%
By that, I mean it was
a county commissioner on Zoo

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:32.532
and we were at the house,
my two children and her brother.

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:35.068 size:84% position:42% line:92%
And we did it that way.

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:38.538 size:78% position:39%
-Actually having had
a very large wedding,

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.907
that actually sounds pretty nice
and low stress.

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:46.880
-Let's say there are sociall
liberating factors under COVID

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:47.781 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:53.887
I want to compliment you on what
I feel was the finest moment

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:54.988 size:88% position:44% line:92%
in television this year.

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:56.056 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
You played yourself

00:00:56.056 --> 00:00:59.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
in an episode
of "Curb Your Enthusiasm"

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:01.428 line:92%
as the owner of a pet store.

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:03.763 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's the hardest I laughed
all year.

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:06.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
Did you enjoy that?
-I did enjoy it, yeah.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:07.634 line:92%
I'm a big fan of that show

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:11.504 align:start size:94% position:6%
so it was nice to get
the invitation to come down.

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:14.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
They have a pretty loose
play plan,

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:18.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
very encouraging,
and we had a lot of fun, yeah.

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:22.148 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Does all the time you're
spending here take away from

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:22.949 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
you know, acting?

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:26.286 size:84% position:42%
-I was never a very Zen
sort of personality.

00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:30.023 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I find myself very,
very calm now.

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:32.025 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think, you know,
it's the birds.

00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:34.194 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Toucan screeches ]
Shut up!

00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:35.295 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Toucan squawks ]

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:37.630 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, I asked Larry David
about it,

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.934 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he said you improvised
yelling at the parrots,

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:44.370 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I thought, well,
that was the best moment,

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.006 align:start size:94% position:6%
so, again, I tip my cap.
-Thanks.

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:50.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
I can't remember what was
improvised and what was not,

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:52.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
because we just did it
a few times

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:55.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they kind of loosened it u
each time.

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:56.549 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And, yeah.

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:58.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I had a good time doing it
Short, you know?

00:01:58.585 --> 00:02:01.855 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's just, uh,
shot one morning and, yeah

00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:04.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's very nice when you -
when you create a world

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:08.862
for someone to come in,
work a very short amount of time

00:02:08.862 --> 00:02:09.596 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and score.

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:12.065
That's why you keep gettin
people to say yes to you.

00:02:12.065 --> 00:02:14.767
-Yeah.
They figured out that chemistry.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:18.238
They've got -- so you go i
there, you kind of feel like

00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:21.007 align:start size:94% position:6%
there is some unseen
safety net anyway

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:23.643
because everybody's terrific
on that show all the time.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:27.647
So, you figure, well, I don'
want to be the odd one out

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:30.483 line:92%
-[ Laughs ] Right? Exactly

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:32.318 line:92%
I want to talk about CORE.

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:34.487 align:start size:94% position:6%
So this is an organization
you started

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:35.922 line:92%
after the earthquake in Haiti.

00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:41.794
And now you're also using it
to help increase COVID testing

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:43.630 align:start size:94% position:6%
what brought you to
the decision to do that,

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.432 align:start size:94% position:6%
and was it easy to take
an organization that had bee

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:49.469 size:84% position:42%
set up for other things
and to sort of shift it

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:50.637 size:88% position:44% line:92%
into this testing world?

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:53.573
-Yes, and no.
I mean, what was easy was to say

00:02:53.573 --> 00:02:56.743 align:start size:94% position:6%
we had more than most
in terms of the infrastructure

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:59.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
of a disaster response
organization.

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:02.515 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we had done infectious
disease once

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:04.317 align:start size:94% position:6%
with the cholera outbreak
in Haiti.

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:07.687 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's, you know, a bit of
a starter kit

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:12.225 size:88% position:44%
for what this incredibly
transmittable COVID is.

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:15.995 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we initially went to
the governor of California

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:17.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
and said,
"Look, we've got people.

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:22.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've got some experience,
and we've got the will to play

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:23.603 line:92%
Where would you guide us?"

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:27.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
He guided us to Mayor Garcetti
in Los Angeles city

00:03:27.607 --> 00:03:29.275 line:92%
and Los Angeles Fire Department,

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:31.811
where they had already set u
test sites, but they were --

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:34.981
those test sites were bein
operated by firefighters

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:39.719 align:start size:94% position:6%
who, frankly, had other things
to be doing for the city.

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:43.790 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so we were able to tak
training and to absorb

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:47.660 align:start size:94% position:6%
those sites and then expan
site after site,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.630 size:78% position:39%
until now we're doing
all the city sites.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:53.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know, one of the majo
issues has been a lack

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.736 size:88% position:44%
of transparency from the
government to the people

00:03:56.736 --> 00:04:00.840
insofar as what -- how long it's
going to take your results

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.376
what it means you need to do i
the time between when you ge

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:04.277 line:92%
tests and you get your results

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:06.913 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is it important when you'r
testing people to let them kno

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:09.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
exactly how long it's going to
be until they find out?

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:10.783 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-I think it's essential.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:13.419 size:84% position:42%
And this is something
that we've been working

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:16.322 align:start size:94% position:6%
very hard with the city on
and in the --

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:18.891 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that's just in
our operations in Los Angeles.

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:21.494 size:88% position:44%
Because, you know, we're
throughout the country.

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:24.330 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we've been expanding thi
with a criteria

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.332 line:92%
for our organization to operate.

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:29.936 align:start size:94% position:6%
Our criteria is moving towards
a total five-day program.

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:33.406 size:84% position:42%
So you are notified --
from the time you test,

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:35.308 line:92%
you come in with the assumptio

00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:37.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you will cooperate
in contact tracing,

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:40.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
you will isolate from the time
you're tested,

00:04:40.647 --> 00:04:42.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
certainly to the time
you are notified.

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:45.585
If you're notified positive,
you will continue to quarantine,

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:49.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you'll be contact traced
all within five days.

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:52.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
After five days, any of thes
reports that you hear,

00:04:52.659 --> 00:04:57.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
if it is more than five days
the cat's out of the bag.

00:04:57.297 --> 00:04:59.632 line:92%
It becomes relatively useless,

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:02.568 align:start size:94% position:6%
except for those who
by being notified

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:06.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
begin to quarantine
from additional spread

00:05:06.172 --> 00:05:07.607 line:92%
with the community or family

00:05:07.607 --> 00:05:11.377
But it's under a -- where
you have an open economy,

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:15.848
very difficult to get people who
are out of a job a long time to,

00:05:15.848 --> 00:05:16.983 line:92%
if they are asymptomatic,

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:18.885 align:start size:94% position:6%
quarantine themselves
out of a job.

00:05:18.885 --> 00:05:21.387 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it has to be --
the language, at least,

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:25.325
has to be charged with
the seriousness of the pandemic.

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:30.363
It is not always enforceable
but it is always mandatory

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:32.398 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's what
we're moving toward,

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:35.401
but we want people to know tha
from the time they're tested

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:40.340 align:start size:94% position:6%
they will only have two days
of full isolation

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.642 line:92%
if they then test negative

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:44.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And do you find, like,
when you explain it

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:47.180
to people in sort of terms
that are easy to understan

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:50.783
like that, that they're pretty
game to go along with the rule

00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:52.385 align:start size:94% position:6%
you set out?
-Yes.

00:05:52.385 --> 00:05:54.487 line:92%
I think that it is less an issue

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:59.225
of rebellion against the
machine or against liberty

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:02.528 align:start size:94% position:6%
than it is an issue
of chaotic communications

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.063 line:92%
from the White House down.

00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:07.433
And also, through a lot of -
While a lot of the media's

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:10.870 size:78% position:39%
done a very good job,
if half of the media

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:14.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
is not doing a good job,
if they're going point to poin

00:06:14.107 --> 00:06:16.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
every day, you lose track
of the five,

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:18.745 size:75% position:38%
six essential things
you've got to know.

00:06:18.745 --> 00:06:21.080 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's those essential thing
you got to demand

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:25.785
of your friends and neighbor
and of the public health system.

00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:29.288 line:92%
-This is very exciting as well

00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:31.891 align:start size:94% position:6%
I know you're doing
a reunion table read

00:06:31.891 --> 00:06:34.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
of "Fast Times
at Ridgemont High"

00:06:34.193 --> 00:06:35.995 align:start size:94% position:6%
coming up in a couple of weeks
right?

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:39.165 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right.
Yeah, August 20th, I think

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:41.200 line:92%
-That is such an iconic role

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
but is it true that
you did not feel as though

00:06:44.337 --> 00:06:46.172 line:92%
you nailed that audition?

00:06:46.172 --> 00:06:48.508 line:92%
-Oh, for the original movie?

00:06:48.508 --> 00:06:49.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:55.481 size:81% position:41%
No, I have to say that
that movie's audition

00:06:55.481 --> 00:06:56.783 align:start size:94% position:6%
gets more attention
and questions,

00:06:56.783 --> 00:07:01.287
but it is true that I never di
a good audition for anything

00:07:01.287 --> 00:07:03.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was just not a good --
-Okay.

00:07:03.423 --> 00:07:06.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
-The big difference with tha
one was after I failed

00:07:06.659 --> 00:07:07.960 align:start size:94% position:6%
the audition,
the casting director,

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.830
Don Phillips, came running after
me in the parking lot,

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:12.698 line:92%
I guess having seen something,

00:07:12.698 --> 00:07:15.868 align:start size:94% position:6%
and drew me in to give it
a little bit more.

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:20.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was resentful of --
of auditions and just felt

00:07:20.706 --> 00:07:22.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
if they didn't get it
by osmosis,

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:24.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
I shouldn't give it till
they were paying me.

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:25.978 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:25.978 --> 00:07:28.514 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, as you've also been
on the other side,

00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:29.248 size:84% position:42% line:92%
you've been a director.

00:07:29.248 --> 00:07:31.818 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel as the directo
when people come and audition,

00:07:31.818 --> 00:07:35.054
you have a great empathy for
what they're going through

00:07:35.054 --> 00:07:38.891 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. And many times
I won't audition people.

00:07:38.891 --> 00:07:42.328 size:84% position:42%
I'll often sort of just
have somebody come in

00:07:42.328 --> 00:07:47.166
and meet and get a sense of them
and talk to them a little bit,

00:07:47.166 --> 00:07:50.002
their instincts about the
character and the story.

00:07:50.002 --> 00:07:55.241 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know, there's bee
times where I've gone and cast

00:07:55.241 --> 00:07:58.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
non-actors without
auditioning them.

00:07:58.144 --> 00:08:00.746
And other times you feel you
have to, 'cause you're looking

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:03.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
for some kind of a chemistry
with somebody.

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:08.054 align:start size:94% position:6%
You got to see that or --
but, yeah, I'm very empathetic

00:08:08.054 --> 00:08:10.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
I find it painful.
-Yes.

00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:14.260 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've been very happy that
the last decade or so

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:15.962 align:start size:94% position:6%
of my life, I haven't had
to go through that,

00:08:15.962 --> 00:08:18.331 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it is --
it is heartbreaking.

00:08:18.331 --> 00:08:21.400
-It's the only good thing
about success in acting,

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:23.202 line:92%
frankly, is not auditioning.

00:08:23.202 --> 00:08:25.872 size:84% position:42%
-Does that mean that --
I know you've directed

00:08:25.872 --> 00:08:26.572 size:78% position:39% line:92%
Jack Nicholson twice.

00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:28.708
is it safe to assume that
he did not have to auditio

00:08:28.708 --> 00:08:29.876 size:75% position:38% line:92%
for either of those?

00:08:29.876 --> 00:08:32.912 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Surprisingly, he did not
have to audition.

00:08:32.912 --> 00:08:35.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
He just had to respond
to my pleas.

00:08:35.615 --> 00:08:40.019 align:start size:94% position:6%
-In the days when
I was struggling at "SNL,"

00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:42.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
I never quite found like
I had my footing.

00:08:42.288 --> 00:08:43.723 align:start size:94% position:6%
I wasn't particularly good
at impressions,

00:08:43.723 --> 00:08:46.959
but I did do a Sean Penn
impression back in the day

00:08:46.959 --> 00:08:48.594 line:92%
and, uh, we've never met.

00:08:48.594 --> 00:08:49.629 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is the first time
we've interacted,

00:08:49.629 --> 00:08:53.733
and you're much better humored
than I feel like I portrayed you

00:08:53.733 --> 00:08:56.702 align:start size:94% position:6%
on the show, so I feel lik
I owe you an apology.

00:08:56.702 --> 00:08:58.571 line:92%
-No, I reject that apology

00:08:58.571 --> 00:09:02.575 align:start size:94% position:6%
I saw exactly myself
at many chapters of life.

00:09:02.575 --> 00:09:04.277 line:92%
It was -- it's a zone I get in

00:09:04.277 --> 00:09:06.812 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's not that I don't find
the moment funny.

00:09:06.812 --> 00:09:09.215 line:92%
I think I actually sometimes -

00:09:09.215 --> 00:09:13.252 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a kind of adrenaline
in following

00:09:13.252 --> 00:09:17.323
one's own self-seriousness
that I guess is a certain,

00:09:17.323 --> 00:09:23.029 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know, counterpoint
that I get quite a kick out of

00:09:23.029 --> 00:09:24.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Chuckles ]
Well, that's good.

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:26.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's good to know that there's
a kick getting

00:09:26.999 --> 00:09:30.803 size:78% position:39%
getting passed along
in the viewing of it.

00:09:30.803 --> 00:09:33.706
Hey, thanks so much not just
for making the time for us

00:09:33.706 --> 00:09:36.108 align:start size:94% position:6%
today, but with everything
you're doing with CORE.

00:09:36.108 --> 00:09:39.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
We really appreciate it.
-Thank you very much.

